NNAAMMEE
       fallocate - preallocate or deallocate space to a file

SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
       ffaallllooccaattee [--cc|--pp|--zz] [--oo _o_f_f_s_e_t] --ll _l_e_n_g_t_h [--nn] _f_i_l_e_n_a_m_e

       ffaallllooccaattee --dd [--oo _o_f_f_s_e_t] [--ll _l_e_n_g_t_h] _f_i_l_e_n_a_m_e

       ffaallllooccaattee --xx [--oo _o_f_f_s_e_t] --ll _l_e_n_g_t_h _f_i_l_e_n_a_m_e

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
       ffaallllooccaattee is used to manipulate the allocated disk space for a file,
       either to deallocate or preallocate it. For filesystems which support
       the ffaallllooccaattee(2) system call, preallocation is done quickly by
       allocating blocks and marking them as uninitialized, requiring no IO to
       the data blocks. This is much faster than creating a file by filling it
       with zeroes.

       The exit status returned by ffaallllooccaattee is 0 on success and 1 on failure.

OOPPTTIIOONNSS
       The _l_e_n_g_t_h and _o_f_f_s_e_t arguments may be followed by the multiplicative
       suffixes KiB (=1024), MiB (=1024*1024), and so on for GiB, TiB, PiB,
       EiB, ZiB, and YiB (the "iB" is optional, e.g., "K" has the same meaning
       as "KiB") or the suffixes KB (=1000), MB (=1000*1000), and so on for
       GB, TB, PB, EB, ZB, and YB.

       The options ----ccoollllaappssee--rraannggee, ----ddiigg--hhoolleess, ----ppuunncchh--hhoollee, and
       ----zzeerroo--rraannggee are mutually exclusive.

       --cc, ----ccoollllaappssee--rraannggee
           Removes a byte range from a file, without leaving a hole. The byte
           range to be collapsed starts at _o_f_f_s_e_t and continues for _l_e_n_g_t_h
           bytes. At the completion of the operation, the contents of the file
           starting at the location _o_f_f_s_e_t+_l_e_n_g_t_h will be appended at the
           location _o_f_f_s_e_t, and the file will be _l_e_n_g_t_h bytes smaller. The
           option ----kkeeeepp--ssiizzee may not be specified for the collapse-range
           operation.

           Available since Linux 3.15 for ext4 (only for extent-based files)
           and XFS.

           A filesystem may place limitations on the granularity of the
           operation, in order to ensure efficient implementation. Typically,
           _o_f_f_s_e_t and _l_e_n_g_t_h must be a multiple of the filesystem logical
           block size, which varies according to the filesystem type and
           configuration. If a filesystem has such a requirement, the
           operation will fail with the error EEIINNVVAALL if this requirement is
           violated.

       --dd, ----ddiigg--hhoolleess
           Detect and dig holes. This makes the file sparse in-place, without
           using extra disk space. The minimum size of the hole depends on
           filesystem I/O block size (usually 4096 bytes). Also, when using
           this option, ----kkeeeepp--ssiizzee is implied. If no range is specified by
           ----ooffffsseett and ----lleennggtthh, then the entire file is analyzed for holes.

           You can think of this option as doing a "ccpp ----ssppaarrssee" and then
           renaming the destination file to the original, without the need for
           extra disk space.

           See ----ppuunncchh--hhoollee for a list of supported filesystems.

       --ii, ----iinnsseerrtt--rraannggee
           Insert a hole of _l_e_n_g_t_h bytes from _o_f_f_s_e_t, shifting existing data.

       --ll, ----lleennggtthh _l_e_n_g_t_h
           Specifies the length of the range, in bytes.

       --nn, ----kkeeeepp--ssiizzee
           Do not modify the apparent length of the file. This may effectively
           allocate blocks past EOF, which can be removed with a truncate.

       --oo, ----ooffffsseett _o_f_f_s_e_t
           Specifies the beginning offset of the range, in bytes.

       --pp, ----ppuunncchh--hhoollee
           Deallocates space (i.e., creates a hole) in the byte range starting
           at _o_f_f_s_e_t and continuing for _l_e_n_g_t_h bytes. Within the specified
           range, partial filesystem blocks are zeroed, and whole filesystem
           blocks are removed from the file. After a successful call,
           subsequent reads from this range will return zeroes. This option
           may not be specified at the same time as the ----zzeerroo--rraannggee option.
           Also, when using this option, ----kkeeeepp--ssiizzee is implied.

           Supported for XFS (since Linux 2.6.38), ext4 (since Linux 3.0),
           Btrfs (since Linux 3.7), tmpfs (since Linux 3.5) and gfs2 (since
           Linux 4.16).

       --vv, ----vveerrbboossee
           Enable verbose mode.

       --xx, ----ppoossiixx
           Enable POSIX operation mode. In that mode allocation operation
           always completes, but it may take longer time when fast allocation
           is not supported by the underlying filesystem.

       --zz, ----zzeerroo--rraannggee
           Zeroes space in the byte range starting at _o_f_f_s_e_t and continuing
           for _l_e_n_g_t_h bytes. Within the specified range, blocks are
           preallocated for the regions that span the holes in the file. After
           a successful call, subsequent reads from this range will return
           zeroes.

           Zeroing is done within the filesystem preferably by converting the
           range into unwritten extents. This approach means that the
           specified range will not be physically zeroed out on the device
           (except for partial blocks at the either end of the range), and I/O
           is (otherwise) required only to update metadata.

           Option ----kkeeeepp--ssiizzee can be specified to prevent file length
           modification.

           Available since Linux 3.14 for ext4 (only for extent-based files)
           and XFS.

       --hh, ----hheellpp
           Display help text and exit.

       --VV, ----vveerrssiioonn
           Print version and exit.

